    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-838",
      "problem": "Simplify $\\dfrac{6y(x - 2)}{9yz}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $6y(x-2)$ in the numerator  becomes $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot (x-2),$ and the $9yz$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot z.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14156876/assets/18f38c8c6d15717bc8c192b3300c7a32ab4b568ea9adeecd7db0b6b5383085ab.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $y$'s.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14156876/assets/27388b8762042ed84e66e616b3bb3ee9524bd34105b2d7bcd1b433cd4850ecfd.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{2(x - 2)}{3z}.$",
